/* area.sym -- the area/map id space.
 *
 * WHAT THIS IS
 *
 * A single signed halfword at gState+0x1C0 holds the current area. Sixty-one
 * hand-written functions read it and compare it against these values to pick
 * per-area data: edge-transition tables, map-entrance tables, event tables and
 * scripts. One id, many lookups.
 *
 * WHY THE OPERANDS HAVE TO BE SYMBOLS
 *
 * gcc-2.96 never pools a constant it can build with an eight-bit `mov`, and
 * always pools a symbol's address. The ROM pools these -- `ldr r3, =0x4d`
 * where `cmp r2, #0x4d` would do -- so they were symbol references in the
 * original source. No spelling of a literal reproduces it. An absolute symbol
 * definition in a linker script emits no bytes, so the link is byte-identical.
 *
 * THE NAMES ARE AN INFERENCE, ADOPTED DELIBERATELY (2026-08-03)
 *
 * These were `_ID_xx` -- named by value, asserting nothing -- while the
 * namespace was unidentified. The evidence for calling it an area id:
 *
 *   * 121 of the 122 ids are compared in exactly ONE overlay, which is what
 *     "each area's code lives in one place" looks like. This is structural,
 *     read out of the ROM, and is the strongest single argument.
 *   * the tables selected are named MapEntrance_ARRAY_*, Events_TolbiSpring,
 *     Events_GameBuildings -- per-location data.
 *   * a ROM annotation reads "area 0x13 -> .L1d04".
 *
 * WHAT THE EVIDENCE IS NOT. The MapEntrance/Events names arrived with the
 * upstream tree and are NOT the maintainer's -- they are an earlier
 * contributor's inference, so they corroborate nothing on their own. The
 * annotation corpus is documented in docs/attribution.md as getting mechanism
 * right and purpose wrong often enough to matter. Genuinely independent
 * support is the overlay-exclusivity result, and that alone.
 *
 * KNOWN ODDITIES a correct account should explain:
 *
 *   * ids 0x00-0x0f never appear.
 *   * the space is 70% dense over 0x10-0xbd -- 52 unused values inside the
 *     range. An earlier note here claimed "contiguous per-area runs"; that was
 *     wrong, 11 of 23 overlays have gaps and two have clusters 56 apart.
 *   * 0x6a is compared in TWO overlays, alone against the other 121.
 *
 * If this turns out to be wrong, the fix is a rename: the values are what
 * matter to the link, and they are unchanged.
 */

_AREA_01 = 0x1;
_AREA_10 = 0x10;
_AREA_13 = 0x13;
_AREA_1d = 0x1d;
